### SpecForge — connect a repository, then browse what it imported
#
# Run against the composed stack: `docker compose up -d` (PostgreSQL on 30301, Keycloak on 8081)
# and the application on 8080. Pick the `dev` environment in the client before sending anything —
# the variables live in `http-client.env.json`.
#
# The requests below are in order and chained: each one stores what the next needs
# (`adminToken`, `installationId`, `scanId`, `connectionId`, `specId`), so run them top to bottom
# the first time. Afterwards any single request can be re-sent on its own.
#
# Steps 2 to 5 are the connection flow, which has no UI yet. Steps 6 onward are the catalogue.

### 1. Sign in
# Keycloak is the only user store — SpecForge issues no credentials of its own. The dev realm
# enables the direct access grant so a token can be had without a browser; a real realm does not.
# The realm ships three users, each with the roles their name implies:
#   reviewer / reviewer        REVIEWER
#   architect / architect      REVIEWER, ARCHITECT
#   admin-user / admin-user    REVIEWER, ADMIN   ← connecting a repository needs this one
POST {{keycloak}}/realms/specforge/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=specforge-web&username={{user}}&password={{password}}

> {%
    client.global.set("token", response.body.access_token);
    client.test("a token came back", () => client.assert(response.status === 200));
%}

### 1b. Who am I
# The local mirror of the token: display name, actor kind and the realm roles. Check that `roles`
# contains ADMIN before trying to connect anything.
GET {{host}}/api/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 1c. A check runner's token
# Agents authenticate through the confidential client's service account, not as a person. Its
# `actorKind` is AGENT — the field that keeps human and automated output distinguishable. Sending
# this overwrites `token`; re-run request 1 to go back to being a human.
POST {{keycloak}}/realms/specforge/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=specforge-api&client_secret=specforge-api-dev-secret

> {% client.global.set("token", response.body.access_token); %}

### 2. The forge installations SpecForge can read through
# Nothing here creates an installation. It arrives over the GitHub App's `installation` webhook
# (request 11), and the repositories it grants are the boundary every read stays inside. An empty
# list means the app is not installed, or `SPECFORGE_GITHUB_APP_ID` / `SPECFORGE_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`
# are unset — without them the forge client refuses every call rather than pretending a repository
# is unreachable.
GET {{host}}/api/forge/installations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    const first = response.body.items[0];
    if (first) {
        client.global.set("installationId", first.id);
    }
%}

### 3. Scan before connecting                                                          [ADMIN]
# What would import, what is a change proposal rather than a specification, and what cannot be
# parsed — answered before anything is created. The scan runs asynchronously; this returns
# immediately with a RUNNING scan.
POST {{host}}/api/forge/scans
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "installationId": "{{installationId}}",
  "repositoryFullName": "{{repositoryFullName}}",
  "branch": "{{branch}}",
  "pathGlob": "{{pathGlob}}",
  "specFormat": "{{specFormat}}"
}

> {% client.global.set("scanId", response.body.id); %}

### 3b. Read the scan
# Re-send until `status` is SUCCEEDED. `files` carries a row per matched path with why it was
# classified as it was, so "why is this file not a specification" is answerable without scanning
# again.
GET {{host}}/api/forge/scans/{{scanId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 4. Connect the repository                                                          [ADMIN]
# Creates the connection and triggers the initial import in one call. Two things are refused here:
# a repository, branch and path already connected (409, never a silent duplicate), and a glob that
# matches nothing importable (422 — a connection that imports nothing is a dead end).
#
# `syncMode` defaults to ON_PULL_REQUEST: a pull request touching a spec is the moment a team wants
# review, and approvals report back as a commit status. ON_PUSH and MANUAL are the alternatives.
#
# The approval rule is stored verbatim and never read by this module; the approval capability
# evaluates it.
POST {{host}}/api/repository-connections
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "installationId": "{{installationId}}",
  "repositoryFullName": "{{repositoryFullName}}",
  "branch": "{{branch}}",
  "pathGlob": "{{pathGlob}}",
  "specFormat": "{{specFormat}}",
  "syncMode": "{{syncMode}}",
  "scanId": "{{scanId}}",
  "project": {
    "name": "{{projectName}}",
    "team": "{{projectTeam}}",
    "tracker": "NONE",
    "approvalRule": {
      "minApprovals": 2,
      "requiredRoles": ["ARCHITECT"]
    }
  }
}

> {% client.global.set("connectionId", response.body.id); %}

### 4b. The connections the caller can see
# A connection whose installation was revoked or suspended shows as DEGRADED with a reason, not
# deleted: losing a grant is not a reason to lose the review history of what the repository held.
GET {{host}}/api/repository-connections
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    const first = response.body.items[0];
    if (first) {
        client.global.set("connectionId", first.id);
    }
%}

### 5. Watch the import
# A row per file including the ones nothing happened to. `importedCount` counts new versions,
# `unchangedCount` the files whose content hash matched what was already stored — re-importing an
# unchanged file from a later commit is deliberately a no-op.
GET {{host}}/api/repository-connections/{{connectionId}}/import-runs
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 5b. Re-import by hand                                                              [ADMIN]
# Independent of the sync mode. Useful after pushing to the branch when sync is ON_PULL_REQUEST.
POST {{host}}/api/repository-connections/{{connectionId}}/imports
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6. Browse the catalogue
# Grouped by project unless `groupBy` says otherwise. The group counts are computed over the whole
# filtered result rather than over the returned page, so they still sum to `total`.
GET {{host}}/api/specs
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    const first = response.body.items[0];
    if (first) {
        client.global.set("specId", first.id);
    }
%}

### 6b. Regroup
# The same specifications, bucketed differently. PROJECT, REPOSITORY, DOMAIN or TEAM.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?groupBy=REPOSITORY
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6c. Filters combine
# Every filter is optional, they apply as a conjunction, and a repeated parameter matches any of
# its values. This asks for specifications that are (in review OR changes requested) AND in the
# billing domain.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?status=IN_REVIEW&status=CHANGES_REQUESTED&domain=billing
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6d. Search
# Titles, repository paths, section headings and body text. Each hit names the section that matched
# and returns a snippet with the terms wrapped in `[[` and `]]` — deliberately not HTML, because
# the snippet is body text from someone else's repository. A heading match outranks a body match.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?q=validation
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6e. Search applies on top of the filters, not instead of them
GET {{host}}/api/specs?q=validation&status=APPROVED&groupBy=TEAM
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6f. Page through
# `cursor` comes from the previous response and is absent on the last page. It is validated on the
# way in — it arrives in a URL, and a URL is edited.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?limit=2
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    if (response.body.cursor) {
        client.global.set("cursor", response.body.cursor);
    }
%}

### 6g. The next page
GET {{host}}/api/specs?limit=2&cursor={{cursor}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 7. Read a specification
# The document's metadata, the current version's markdown body and outline, and every version that
# can be selected — one response, so opening a document is one request rather than three. The body
# is markdown; SpecForge never emits HTML.
GET {{host}}/api/specs/{{specId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 7b. Read an older version
# `current` is false and the version selector still lists all of them. An ordinal that does not
# exist is a 404.
GET {{host}}/api/specs/{{specId}}?version=1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 8. The projects overview
# Per project: its connected repositories, counts per lifecycle status, and open reviews. Built
# from the imported specifications themselves, so a project appears once it holds one.
GET {{host}}/api/projects
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 9. Editing a specification is refused — always 409
# The connected repository is the source of truth and SpecForge is a read-only mirror. The problem
# document names the repository and path, directing the author to change the file in git. This
# endpoint exists to say so in the contract rather than to leave the rule implicit.
PUT {{host}}/api/specs/{{specId}}/content
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": "# Billing\n\nEdited in SpecForge, which is not a thing.\n"
}

### 10. The contract itself
# The bundled document the server generates from `src/main/resources/openapi/`, not something a
# scanner reconstructed from annotations.
GET {{host}}/api/openapi.json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 11. A GitHub webhook delivery
# Every delivery is HMAC-verified against `SPECFORGE_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET` before anything is
# parsed — an unverified delivery is an unauthenticated trigger for imports and outbound writes.
# The signature below is a placeholder, so this returns 401 until it is replaced with a real
# `sha256=` HMAC of the exact body bytes. Copy a real one out of the GitHub App's delivery log.
POST {{host}}/api/webhooks/github
X-GitHub-Event: installation
X-GitHub-Delivery: 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=replace-me
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "action": "created",
  "installation": {
    "id": 1,
    "account": { "login": "acme", "type": "Organization" }
  }
}

### 12. No token at all — 401
# Every endpoint is authenticated; there is no anonymous read.
GET {{host}}/api/specs
